<main data-pagefind-body="" id="main"><section class="reference-intro" id="top"><nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="breadcrumb"><a href="/index.html">Home</a><span>/</span><span>Sources &amp; References</span></nav><p class="eyebrow">THE REFERENCE LIBRARY</p><h1>Sources<br/><em>&amp; References.</em></h1><p>Texts, translations and commentaries behind the pages of Sanatan Heritage.</p></section><div class="reference-layout"><aside class="reference-contents"><p class="eyebrow">FIND A PAGE</p><nav aria-label="Reference sections"><a href="#home">Home</a><a href="#understanding">Understanding Sanatan Dharma</a><a href="#deities">Gods &amp; Goddesses</a><a href="#scriptures">Sacred Texts &amp; Scriptures</a><a href="#stories">The Story Collection</a><a href="#lalita-bhandasura">Lalita and Bhandasura</a><a href="#lalita">Lalita Tripurasundari</a><a href="#glossary">Glossary &amp; reading paths</a><a href="#shiva-parvati">Shiva and Parvati</a><a href="#shiva-halahala">Shiva drinks Halahala</a></nav></aside><div class="reference-sections"><section class="reference-section" id="home"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Home</h2><a href="/index.html">Return to page</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p>Introductory sources for the meaning, concepts, traditions and sacred literature of Sanatan Dharma.</p><div class="source-list"><a href="https://www.hinduamerican.org/hinduism-basics" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><span>Hindu American Foundation</span><small>Hinduism Basics</small></a><a href="https://www.himalayanacademy.com/media/books/what-is-hinduism/web/ch12a.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><span>Himalayan Academy</span><small>What Is Hinduism? · Questions &amp; answers</small></a><a href="https://www.swami-krishnananda.org/brdup/Brihadaranyaka_Upanishad.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><span>The Brihadaranyaka Upanishad</span><small>Swami Krishnananda · Text &amp; commentary (PDF)</small></a></div></div></section><section class="reference-section" id="understanding"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Understanding Sanatan Dharma</h2><a href="/understanding.html">Return to page</a></div><div class="reference-content"><ol><li id="source-1"><a href="https://www.hinduamerican.org/learn/hinduism-faq" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Hindu American Foundation · Short Answers to Real Questions</a><small>Meaning of Sanatana Dharma; dharma, atman, Brahman and moksha.</small></li><li id="source-2"><a href="https://www.hinduamerican.org/hinduism-basics" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Hindu American Foundation · Hinduism Basics</a><small>Sacred literature, philosophical schools and diversity.</small></li><li id="source-3"><a href="https://www.hinduamerican.org/hinduism-101-basics" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Hindu American Foundation · Hinduism 101</a><small>Introductions to karma, samsara and moksha.</small></li><li id="source-4"><a href="https://www.hinduamerican.org/hinduism-101/how" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Hindu American Foundation · Paths and Practice</a><small>Devotion, knowledge, selfless service and meditation.</small></li><li id="source-5"><a href="https://www.himalayanacademy.com/media/books/what-is-hinduism/web/intro.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Himalayan Academy · What Is Hinduism?</a><small>A Shaiva organisation’s introduction to the wider tradition.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.gitasupersite.iitk.ac.in/srimad?language=dv&amp;field_chapter_value=2&amp;field_nsutra_value=47&amp;etsiva=1" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IIT Kanpur · Bhagavad Gita 2.47</a><small>Primary verse, translations and commentaries.</small></li></ol></div></section><section class="reference-section" id="deities"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Gods &amp; Goddesses</h2><a href="/deities.html">Return to page</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p>Religious organisations explain their traditions from within; museum essays discuss sacred art. The Upanishad source includes Swami Krishnananda’s commentary.</p><ol><li id="dsource-1"><a href="https://www.himalayanacademy.com/media/books/four-denominations-of-hinduism_ei/web/ch02.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Himalayan Academy · Four Denominations</a><small>Divine traditions; a Shaiva organisation’s perspective.</small></li><li id="dsource-2"><a href="https://www.hinduamerican.org/learn/hinduism-faq" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Hindu American Foundation · Hinduism FAQ</a><small>Deity traditions and the feminine Divine.</small></li><li id="dsource-3"><a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/hinduism-and-hindu-art" rel="noopener" target="_blank">The Met · Hinduism and Hindu Art</a><small>Vidya Dehejia · Divine forms and sacred imagery.</small></li><li id="dsource-4"><a href="https://www.himalayanacademy.com/media/books/my-friend-lord-ganesha_ei/web/ch23a.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Himalayan Academy · My Friend, Lord Ganesha</a><small>Ganesha in devotional practice.</small></li><li id="dsource-5"><a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/es/perspectives/household-gods-kali-lithography" rel="noopener" target="_blank">The Met · Seeing Kali Clearly</a><small>Goddess imagery and devotional context.</small></li><li id="dsource-6"><a href="https://www.swami-krishnananda.org/brdup/brhad_III-09.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Brihadaranyaka Upanishad · Chapter 3.9</a><small>Many Gods and One Brahman · Swami Krishnananda.</small></li><li id="dsource-7"><a href="https://www.hinduamerican.org/blog/5-things-to-know-about-hanuman" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Hindu American Foundation · Hanuman</a><small>Hanuman, Rama and the Ramayana.</small></li><li id="dsource-8"><a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/24305" rel="noopener" target="_blank">The Met · Trimurti and Vishnu’s Avatars</a><small>Collection commentary on a South Indian katar.</small></li><li id="dsource-9"><a href="https://www.hinduamerican.org/hinduism-101-basics" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Hindu American Foundation · Hinduism 101</a><small>Krishna’s teachings in the Bhagavad Gita.</small></li></ol></div></section><section class="reference-section" id="scriptures"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Sacred Texts &amp; Scriptures</h2><a href="/scriptures.html">Return to page</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p>Introductory sources for the Vedas, Upanishads, Gita, epics, Puranas, Agamas and Tantras.</p><ol><li id="ssource-1"><a href="https://www.hinduamerican.org/hinduism-basics" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Hindu American Foundation · Key Sources of Knowledge</a><small>Shruti, Smriti, epics and Puranas.</small></li><li id="ssource-2"><a href="https://www.baps.org/About-BAPS/WhoWeAre/BasicBeliefs/ThePhilosophy/HinduBeliefs/BeliefintheRevealedScriptures.aspx" rel="noopener" target="_blank">BAPS · Belief in the Revealed Scriptures</a><small>Vedic literature and oral learning; a Swaminarayan perspective.</small></li><li id="ssource-3"><a href="https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/tradition-of-vedic-chanting-00062" rel="noopener" target="_blank">UNESCO · Tradition of Vedic Chanting</a><small>The four Vedas and their oral preservation.</small></li><li id="ssource-4"><a href="https://www.dlshq.org/teachings/tantra-yoga/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Divine Life Society · Tantra Yoga</a><small>Agamic and Tantric literature from a traditional teaching perspective.</small></li><li id="ssource-5"><a href="https://www.dlshq.org/download/bhagavad-gita/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Divine Life Society · Bhagavad Gita</a><small>Swami Sivananda’s translation and commentary.</small></li></ol></div></section><section class="reference-section" id="stories"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>The Story Collection</h2><a href="/stories.html">Return to page</a></div><div class="reference-content"><ol class="reference-list"><li><strong>Valmiki Ramayana</strong><small>Ralph T. H. Griffith, English verse translation (1870–1874); Y. V. Chalapathi, English prose edition, Tirumala Tirupati Devasthanams (2005).</small></li><li><strong>The Puranas</strong><small>Skanda and Brahmanda Purana editions published by Motilal Banarsidass; F. E. Pargiter’s Markandeya Purana translation (1904); Gita Press’s Sanskrit–Hindi Srimad Bhagavata Mahapurana.</small></li></ol><div class="preview-reference-list"><p id="ref-shiva-poison"><strong>Shiva drinks Halahala</strong><span>Bhagavata Purana · Skandha 8 · Chapter 7</span><a href="#shiva-halahala">Chapter references and editions</a></p><p id="ref-rama-exile"><strong>Rama leaves Ayodhya</strong><span>Valmiki Ramayana · Ayodhya Kanda</span></p><p id="ref-hanuman-lanka"><strong>Hanuman’s leap to Lanka</strong><span>Valmiki Ramayana · Sundara Kanda</span></p><p id="ref-govardhana"><strong>Krishna lifts Govardhana</strong><span>Srimad Bhagavata Purana · Skandha 10 · Chapters 24–27</span></p><p id="ref-mahishasura"><strong>Devi and Mahishasura</strong><span>Markandeya Purana · Devi Mahatmya · Mahishasura narrative</span></p><p id="ref-lalita-bhandasura"><strong>Lalita and Bhandasura</strong><span>Brahmanda Purana · Lalita Mahatmya</span></p><p id="ref-bhagiratha-ganga"><strong>Bhagiratha brings the Ganga</strong><span>Valmiki Ramayana · Bala Kanda · Ganga descent narrative</span></p><p id="ref-shiva-parvati"><strong>Shiva and Parvati</strong><span>Shiva Purana · Rudra Samhita · Parvati Khanda</span><a href="#shiva-parvati">Chapter references and edition</a></p></div></div></section><section class="reference-section" id="lalita-bhandasura"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Lalita and Bhandasura</h2><a href="/lalita-bhandasura.html">Return to page</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>The Brahmanda Purana, Part IV.</strong> Translated and annotated by Dr. G. V. Tagare. Motilal Banarsidass. Lalita Mahatmya, chapters 11–18 and 26–30. Printed pages 1073–1106 and 1168–1221.</p><p>The chapter-and-verse references below identify the main passages used. The commanders’ episode is condensed; the intervening campaign chapters contain additional battles and divine figures.</p><details><summary>Passages behind the key moments<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><ul><li><strong>Bhanda’s origin:</strong> 11.25–38.</li><li><strong>Lalita’s manifestation and promise:</strong> 12.59–75; 13.29–36.</li><li><strong>The royal union and march:</strong> chapters 14–18.</li><li><strong>Bala:</strong> 26.72–117.</li><li><strong>Ganesha and the obstacle:</strong> 27.24–76; 27.102–104.</li><li><strong>The commanders:</strong> chapter 28.</li><li><strong>The final duel:</strong> 29.55–144.</li><li><strong>Recovery and Kama’s restoration:</strong> 29.145–146; 30.4–6; 30.31–56.</li></ul></details><p>Names are written in a simplified spelling for readability. Bhanda and Bhandasura refer to the same figure; Kama, Madana and Manmatha name the god of love. Mantrini is also called Shyamala, and Dandanatha is also called Dandini.</p><p>Images are devotional interpretations accompanying the story. Devotional artworks retain their visible artist or website marks; the Ganesha illustration is AI-generated.</p><a href="/scriptures.html#puranas">Explore the Puranas</a><p>Tagare’s notes to chapter 29, printed pages 1202–1203, interpret the opposing missiles as harmful and beneficial forces. The narrative itself describes divine weapons and their effects.</p></div></section><section class="reference-section" id="lalita"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Lalita Tripurasundari</h2><a href="/lalita.html">Return to page</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p>The descriptions of the Goddess follow the Lalita Mahatmya. The discussion of inward contemplation follows the ArunaLahari commentary.</p><ol><li id="lsource-1"><strong>The Brahmanda Purana · Part IV</strong><small>Dr. G. V. Tagare, translator and annotator; Motilal Banarsidass. Lalita Mahatmya 12.70–75 (appearance); 13.17–27 (supreme reality); chapters 14–18 (union and march); 30.11 and 30.28 (Tripura, Kameshvari and bindu).</small><a href="#lalita-bhandasura">Story passages and reading notes</a></li><li id="lsource-2"><strong>Bhavanopanishad · ArunaLahari commentary</strong><small>“Dravidacharya” Sri Ramakrishnan Swamiji; Shashtra Nethralaya, Rishikesh. Printed pages 3–7: Mahatripurasundari and the Self; modes of worship; Sri Yantra contemplation; the teacher. The introduction is paraphrased on the Lalita page.</small></li><li id="lsource-3"><strong>Srividyarnavatantram · five-part edition</strong><small>Sanskrit text with Hindi commentary by Sri Kapildev Narayan; Chaukhamba Surbharati Prakashan, 2012. Further reading on Sri Vidya.</small></li></ol></div></section><section class="reference-section" id="glossary"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Glossary &amp; reading paths</h2><a href="/glossary.html">Return to glossary</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p>Definitions are brief introductions; meanings vary with philosophical school and context. Pronunciation hints are approximate English guides, alongside Sanskrit transliteration.</p><ol><li><a href="https://www.himalayanacademy.com/media/books/gurus-wisdom/web/glossary.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Himalayan Academy · Guru’s Wisdom glossary</a><small>Terms of practice and philosophy, presented from a Shaiva perspective.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.hinduamerican.org/learn/hinduism-faq" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Hindu American Foundation · Hinduism FAQ</a><small>Dharma, atman, Brahman and liberation.</small></li></ol><p>Reading paths curate the pages already in the collection. Their story sources are listed under The Story Collection above.</p></div></section><section class="reference-section" id="shiva-parvati"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Shiva and Parvati</h2><a href="/shiva-parvati.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Shiva Purana · Rudra Samhita · Parvati Khanda (section 2.3).</strong> English edition credited to J. L. Shastri, available as a chapter-by-chapter transcription at Wisdom Library. Chapter numbers below refer to that edition.</p><p>This is a condensed retelling, not a literal translation or a complete rendering of the Khanda. Extended dialogues, additional divine tests and intervening ceremonies are omitted. Names use simplified spellings; Mena is also written Menā, and Himavat is also called Himachala. All three illustrations are AI-generated devotional interpretations.</p><ol><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226070.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Parvati Khanda · Chapter 6</a><small>The Goddess is born as Parvati.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226072.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Parvati Khanda · Chapter 8</a><small>Narada speaks with Himavat.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226083.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Parvati Khanda · Chapter 19</a><small>Kama is reduced to ashes.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226085.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Parvati Khanda · Chapter 21</a><small>Narada guides Parvati.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226086.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Parvati Khanda · Chapter 22</a><small>Parvati undertakes tapas.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226088.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Parvati Khanda · Chapter 24</a><small>The devas’ appeal and Shiva’s consent.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226089.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Parvati Khanda · Chapter 25</a><small>The seven sages test Parvati.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226090.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Parvati Khanda · Chapter 26</a><small>The ascetic visitor.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226091.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Parvati Khanda · Chapter 27</a><small>The visitor criticises Shiva.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226092.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Parvati Khanda · Chapter 28</a><small>Parvati’s answer and Shiva’s revelation.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226093.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Parvati Khanda · Chapter 29</a><small>Parvati requests marriage with her family’s involvement.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226096.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Parvati Khanda · Chapter 32</a><small>Shiva sends the sages as emissaries.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226104.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Parvati Khanda · Chapter 40</a><small>The wedding procession.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226107.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Parvati Khanda · Chapter 43</a><small>Mena sees the procession.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226109.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Parvati Khanda · Chapter 45</a><small>Shiva’s radiant form.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226112.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Parvati Khanda · Chapter 48</a><small>The marriage ceremony.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226114.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Parvati Khanda · Chapter 50</a><small>The concluding rites and celebration.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226115.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Parvati Khanda · Chapter 51</a><small>Rati’s appeal and Kama’s restoration.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226119.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Parvati Khanda · Chapter 55</a><small>The farewell and return to Kailasa.</small></li></ol><p>The tradition note follows the divine dialogue in chapter 29, especially verses 19–25 and 30–34. The contrast between stillness and active devotion is an editorial reflection. Kama’s restoration is told in chapter 51; the separate Lalita account appears in the Brahmanda Purana’s Lalita Mahatmya, chapter 30.</p></div></section><section class="reference-section" id="shiva-halahala"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Shiva drinks Halahala</h2><a href="/shiva-halahala.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Primary account: Bhagavata Purana · Skandha 8 · Chapter 7.</strong> A condensed retelling in original English, checked against the Sanskrit and the Bhaktivedanta Book Trust translation available at VedaBase. Preparation is introduced in 8.6.22–23; the resumption of churning appears in 8.8.1. Extended prayers and theological dialogue are condensed.</p><p>The Goddess is addressed as Bhavani; 8.7.36 also calls her Sati. The narrative uses Bhavani consistently. Verse 41 describes her consent, and verse 43 the blue mark on Shiva’s throat.</p><ol><li><a href="https://vedabase.io/en/library/sb/8/6/22-23/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">8.6.22–23 · Mandara and Vasuki</a><small>The mountain as rod and serpent as rope; the nectar undertaking.</small></li><li><a href="https://vedabase.io/en/library/sb/8/7/8/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">8.7.8 · The tortoise form</a><small>Vishnu enters the waters and supports the mountain.</small></li><li><a href="https://vedabase.io/en/library/sb/8/7/18/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">8.7.18 · Halahala emerges</a><small>The troubled ocean and the dangerous poison.</small></li><li><a href="https://vedabase.io/en/library/sb/8/7/19/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">8.7.19–20 · Refuge in Shiva</a><small>The poison spreads; the appeal to Shiva seated with the Goddess on Kailasa.</small></li><li><a href="https://vedabase.io/en/library/sb/8/7/36/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">8.7.36–38 · Compassion and protection</a><small>Shiva’s response to suffering and his conversation with Bhavani.</small></li><li><a href="https://vedabase.io/en/library/sb/8/7/41/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">8.7.41 · Bhavani’s consent</a><small>The Goddess understands Shiva’s power.</small></li><li><a href="https://vedabase.io/en/library/sb/8/7/42/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">8.7.42–44 · Drinking the poison</a><small>The act of protection, blue throat and the theme of care for others.</small></li><li><a href="https://vedabase.io/en/library/sb/8/8/1/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">8.8.1 · The churning resumes</a><small>Devas and asuras continue the larger undertaking.</small></li></ol><p><strong>Comparative account: Skanda Purana · Maheshvara Khanda · Kedara Khanda, chapters 9–10.</strong> G. V. Tagare’s English translation is transcribed at Wisdom Library. The Bhagavata and Skanda present distinct theological settings; the tradition note identifies their differences without combining the two narratives.</p><ol><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/the-skanda-purana/d/doc365956.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Kedara Khanda · Chapter 9</a><small>The churning, Narada’s warning and the spreading poison.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/the-skanda-purana/d/doc365957.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Kedara Khanda · Chapter 10</a><small>Shiva, Ganesha and the Shaiva theological setting.</small></li></ol><p><strong>Illustrations:</strong> the Shiva-and-Bhavani hero and ocean-churning scene are AI-generated devotional interpretations created for this story. The Shiva portrait is reused from the deity collection. Images interpret the narrative artistically; they are not historical photographs. The reflection on strength in service is editorial contemplation.</p></div></section></div></div></main>

